getting-in:
The front page has two tabs: **Sign In** and **New Player**.

- **New Player** asks for a username and a password (twice). The button says **Claim your seat**. There is no email and no password reset, so choose something you will remember. You can *change* your password from inside the browser: click your name in the top bar to open the Account window. The command-line client has the same verb, `keryx password` — see [[keryx]].
- **Sign In** takes your username and password.

## Joining a world {#getting-in-details}

After signing in you land on the join page, which names the world and how many Wanaxes are already in it. Press **Enter the World** and you are in — as a [[horde|Nomadic Host]], not as a city.

## Waiting for a world to begin {#getting-in-a-world-that-has-not-begun}

A new world stands still until enough Wanaxes have entered it. Until then no game day passes: nothing is produced, eaten or delivered — and no orders are taken. You can look around, read this Codex and file reports, but every order is refused with *"The world has not begun — 1 of 4 Wanaxes have arrived"* (the numbers are the world's own) until the last of them enters. This is deliberate, so that whoever arrives first does not grow on everyone else's waiting time.

The **Notifications** drawer (☍) tells you whether the world has begun and how many more Wanaxes it is waiting for. If the date in the top bar never changes, this is almost certainly why. See [[time]].

## Signing in again {#getting-in-if-you-are-signed-out}

A session does not last forever. If an action answers that your session has expired, reload the page and sign in again — nothing in the world is lost.

time:
The world advances one **game day** at a time. Durations use **game days**. Arrival and completion times use **your local clock**. The same number of game days can take different real time in different worlds.

## Game days and real time {#time-game-days-and-the-clock}

A march might take **8 game days**, with an estimate such as **today 18:34** beside it. Food supplies show how many game days they will last. Estimates follow the world's current pace and can change if it pauses or its pace changes.

The top bar shows the world's calendar; the sun and moon show the time in the world.

## A world that keeps running {#time-the-world-does-not-wait-for-you}

Production, meals, [[upkeep]], marches and [[battle|battles]] continue while you are away. Leave enough [[food]] and check [[coming-back|Notifications]] when you return.

## Orders before world start {#time-before-the-world-begins}

A new world waits for enough Wanaxes to join. You can look around, but orders become available when it starts. See [[getting-in]].

food:
Your people eat **[[grain|grain]], [[fish|fish]] and [[oil|oil]]** every game day. Wine is not food: it is the drink of [[gods|the gods]] and the feast ([[wine]]). One unit of each supplies one unit of food, and each person eats **0.005 food per game day**: a city of 1 000 eats 5 a game day. <!-- src: server/internal/economy/recompute.go GrainConsumptionPerCitizenPerTick --> The meal is shared in proportion to what is available, including the game day’s production: a city with 100 grain and 900 fish takes one tenth of its meal as grain and nine tenths as fish. No food good is used up first. If these three cannot feed the people, [[livestock|livestock]] remains their emergency food: each animal supplies 3 food. Armies, ships and caravans use the three food goods, not livestock.

## Civilian hunger {#food-when-there-is-not-enough}

If the available food and emergency livestock run out and the game day's need is still unmet:

- the settlement **loses 0.5% of its people that game day** (at least one), and keeps losing while the shortfall lasts;
- it shrinks until what its land yields can feed those who are left — the ground right around a city always feeds about a hundred;
- below **50 people** — starved there, or emptied by drafting soldiers — **the settlement collapses and is gone.**

You get an urgent **CivilianStarvationStarted** notification when hunger is first observed. It records the game day, city and how much of that game day's meal was missing, in food. The latest start stays above ordinary history in Notifications until that city's food supply has recovered steadily. The warning clears after **5 consecutive fully fed game days**. You get **CivilianStarvationEnded**; the old start then returns to ordinary history. Hunger returning during that recovery window belongs to the same warning. A new spell after recovery starts a new pair of notices, rather than repeating the warning every game day.

### Civilian and garrison hunger warnings {#food-when-there-is-not-enough-2}

Civilian meals and [[upkeep|soldiers’ food]] draw from the same stocks, but are served separately: a city's people may be fed while its garrison is hungry, or the other way round. Each warning clears independently. If a city leaves your rule or ceases to be active, its warning closes without claiming its people recovered.

Older **FoodShortfall** messages remain in history. The world's daily food audit still records every shortfall; new inbox messages only mark the transitions. Check **City → Food details** for the current situation and act before the city keeps shrinking.

## Herds and emergency food {#food-the-herd-and-the-pen}

The herd breeds itself — no herder is needed. Every ten animals give one calf every **5 game days** when the city has grain to feed it (**1 grain per calf**), and every **20 game days** on pasture alone. If the grain does not reach every calf, the fed ones come on the 5-game day beat and the rest wait for the 20-game day beat. A city can only keep so many animals: the **herd cap** is 50, and every level of a **[[pen]]** ([[buildings]]) adds 100. **Status** and **City → Food** show it as *Herd 50 / 50*, with the next calves ("4 calves in 3 game days (4 grain)"). Each animal is worth **3 food**. Animals **above** the cap are eaten **first** at the game day's meal — fresh meat does not keep — and the three food goods share whatever hunger is left. If animals are still above the cap after the meal, the pen cannot hold them and they are **lost**. A bigger Pen keeps more of them. Animals under the cap stay in the herd and are eaten only if the people would otherwise starve.

## Responding to a food shortage {#food-what-to-do-about-it}

- Put more [[citizens]] on grain or fish hexes. Check the *next citizen would add* figure.
- Build or level a **[[farm]]** ([[buildings]]) — it raises how many people a grain hex can hold.
- Bring food in from another of your cities by [[transfers|transfer]] or a [[routes|standing order]].
- Buy it ([[trade]]).
- Shrink what eats: soldiers are drafted from your people and eat like them ([[upkeep]]).

Your city has food stocks: the grain, fish and oil in its [[goods|stores]]. **Food for N game days** shows those stocks at current consumption, without counting future production. **City → More → Food details** shows production, the last meal by good and army food separately. Different goods forming a real share of the meal improve [[growth|population growth]]; merely storing another food does not. Stored food buys time during a shortfall. A city under [[sieges|siege]] starves because its fields are cut off, not because it produces too little.

Growth is covered in [[growth]].

incense:
**Incense is the smoke [[gods|the gods]] like best, and it grows in very few places.** It is a rare religious good: no building makes it, no recipe needs it, and nothing in your city fails without it. What it does is make an offering count for more.

## Incense sources {#incense-where-it-comes-from}

Incense is found only on marked hexes of dry scrub ([[maquis|maquis]]) and [[semi-desert|semi-desert]] — the ground that gives almost nothing else. A marked hex shows a small amber drop on the map and the word **Incense** in its inspect panel. [[rites|Potnia's Vision]] can mark an incense source you neither see nor remember; it creates memory, never living eyes. `keryx cities` includes incense among the deposits of known cities. A deposit in your [[catchment]] needs no building: **two citizens** placed on the hex ([[citizens]]) gather it. There are about ten such places in a world of the usual size, scattered over both halves of the map and more of them in the east. Most Wanaxes will have none, which is why it is carried and traded ([[trade]]).

## Incense at the altar {#incense-what-it-does-at-the-altar}

Any good can be offered, and [[wine|wine]] and [[oil|oil]] are offerings too ([[rites]]) — oil is also food, so a hungry city should keep it. For everyday prayers, incense can help meet the offering requirement, but more offering never improves the answer. For older prayers with uncertain answers, **a gift made mostly of incense can please the gods further than any other gift**: it raises the limit on what a larger offering can achieve. It never replaces your standing with the gods ([[kharis]]) — a god who does not hear you is not bought — but it is the best use of what you carry. Each god's taste is shown in the Offer row and in `keryx rite --list`; the gods of sight and omens love it most.

## Frankincense and myrrh {#incense-some-history}

[[bronze|Bronze]] Age temples burned resin, not one perfume. Around the east of the Mediterranean the common kind was the sap of the terebinth tree (*Pistacia*), gathered on the dry [[hills|hills]] of Kna'an and Cyprus and shipped in jars. The wreck at Uluburun, sunk off the south coast of Anatolia at the end of the thirteenth century before our era, carried about a hundred and fifty such jars among its [[copper|copper]] and [[tin|tin]]. Amphorae from Kna'an turn up in Egypt with traces of the same resin. The rarer frankincense and myrrh came to Egypt from Punt, far down the Red Sea, in small and precious loads; labdanum, scraped from the rock-rose shrubs of Crete, was the Minoan smell. Here they are all one good, **Incense**.

xenia:
The [[gods|gods]] keep an order among the Wanaxes even when they are rivals. The Greeks of a later age called it **themis**: what is right because the gods hold it so. Its oldest part is **xenia**, the bond between host and guest.

## Themis {#xenia-themis-what-the-gods-hold}

A few things are not yours to choose. They are not treaties and no one signs them; they are how the world works.

## The sacred messenger {#xenia-the-messenger-is-sacred}

A herald cannot be attacked. No army, raider or Wanax can intercept a messenger, on the way out or on the way home. A letter always reaches its city unless the sea itself takes the [[runners|runner]]. See [[messengers]].

## A herald’s right of passage {#xenia-we-carry-each-other-s-messengers}

A runner who reaches a harbour without a ship of his own boards whatever ship sails his way, **yours or another Wanax's**. No one refuses a herald passage. See [[messengers]] and [[sea]].

## Hospitality for ships {#xenia-the-guest-is-fed}

When a ship reaches a city, its own or another Wanax's, the city gives it food for the voyage home. It is free and needs no consent. The port first keeps enough for its own people's daily meal and gives only what it can spare. See [[ship-food-return]].

## Hiketeia: protection for survivors {#xenia-the-suppliant-hiketeia}

When a Wanax's last city falls and they [[last-city|rise again]], their survivors are suppliants, under the gods' protection until they found a city. You may still attack their [[horde|Nomadic Host]] or its escort. If a [[battle|battle]] against them actually begins, **your realm loses 70 [[kharis|kharis]]** (never below the floor of 1). A march that never reaches them costs nothing. Once they have founded a city, the protection ends.

Before an order against a host you can see goes out, you are warned: in the march panel, **March anyway** sends it; in [[keryx|Keryx]], add `--confirm` to `keryx unit march`. No one else is told; your own kharis shows the loss in **Kult**.

contact:
You cannot write to, or trade with, a city you have never reached. A settlement becomes reachable — **permanently** — once one of these has happened:

- you own it;
- **one of your messengers has physically arrived there**, ever — even if it has long since come home;
- one of your marches has passed over it.

Contact is earned by walking, and once earned it never lapses. Sending a messenger to a stranger's city is not only a letter — it is the act that opens [[trade]] with them for good.

## Permanent contact with a city {#contact-details}

**Rumour is not contact.** [[gossip|Gossip]] tells you that a city exists and roughly where, deliberately blurred, but it does not open the door. Seeing a city on the map ([[sight]]) does not either.

Cities you have contacted appear in **Diplomacy → Letters → Write**. Choose **Write** to ask their Wanax what they need or can spare; market knowledge comes through [[messengers|correspondence]]. A city's [[market|market board]] tells you whom to ask.

Contact reveals that city’s location, not the surrounding land or its current activity.

**A letter you receive tells you who wrote, not where they live.** It opens no contact and puts nothing on your map. You can still answer it — your reply goes home with the same [[runners|runner]] while it waits.

Opening a letter in an exchange marks it as read. The Diplomacy badge counts unread letters; an unanswered offer can remain pending after you have read it. `keryx inbox` marks the letters it displays as read too. Reading does not send the messenger home or accept a trade.

### Keeping and reading received letters {#contact-details-2}

Received letters remain in Letters after the runner leaves or an offer closes. Use `keryx inbox --history` to read past correspondence; plain `keryx inbox` lists current messages and pending offers.

messengers:
A **messenger** walks your letter to another Wanax and brings the answer home. **Diplomacy (✉)** has two tabs: **Letters** and **Runners**.

## Letters between Wanaxes {#messengers-your-letters}

**Letters** has one row per Wanax: their name, the last line of the latest letter, and **Waiting on you**, **Waiting on them** or **Done**. Letters awaiting your answer come first, with the nearest deadline first. An unanswered offer remains actionable even when you cannot afford its payment.

Open a Wanax to read your exchange, oldest first. The newest letter is open; expand an older letter to read it. Opening a letter marks it read. Reading never accepts a trade or sends an answer. Offers have **Accept** and **Decline** buttons. An accepted offer remains in the exchange as **Agreed**, with its exact terms; agreement does not prove the goods have arrived.

### Replying with their Runner {#messengers-your-letters-2}

The reply box sits below the exchange. **Send with their runner** uses the bearer waiting for your answer and shows when he leaves. **Send a runner** sends a new letter to a city you have contacted. **Write** lists the Wanax you can reach, with their contacted cities beneath. A rumour does not open a route. You can also write from a known city's Inspect panel. A letter written there can also **tell of a place** your people have seen; see [[telling-of-places]]. Unsent text stays in the box in this browser while you use the drawer; it is not saved to the server.

Keryx: `letters` lists your correspondents; `letters "Myrine"` reads your exchange with that Wanax. `--json` returns the same data. `reply --id <letter-id> --text "..."` answers with the waiting bearer. `message --to <city> --text "..."` sends a new runner; `trade-offer` attaches structured trade terms. `inbox` remains available for received letters and marks those displayed as read.

## The journey of a letter {#messengers-letters-take-time}

A letter to a distant city takes game days, and the reply takes as long again. You are told when your messenger arrives, and again when he is home with the answer. Plan diplomacy the way you plan a march.

A reply uses the **same bearer** who brought the letter. You can return a letter even if you have not discovered the sender's city; answering does not create a new messenger or reveal that city's location.

A newly delivered plain letter can be answered on its arrival game day and the following game day. The bearer leaves at the start of the next game day. **Letters** shows the departure time with the local departure time. Once the return-post window closes, you cannot reply through that bearer, even while his departure is still being processed. Reading does not extend his stay. A previously issued longer stay keeps its saved deadline. [[trade]] keep their own answer deadline.

### A Runner’s stay at court {#messengers-letters-take-time-2}

If nobody replies, your messenger does not wait forever in that city's court. After a while he gives up and turns around on his own, walking home just as he would with an answer in hand — the same road, taking the same time. You are still told the moment he is back, only without a reply riding along.

## Safety and return of letters {#messengers-messengers-are-sacred}

**A messenger cannot be intercepted** — not on the way out, not on the way home. A herald is sacred and everyone knows it. It is the one thing on the map that is immune, which is why letters are the reliable backbone of everything you do at a distance. See [[xenia]] for [[gods|the gods]]' order behind it.

A [[runners|Runner]] carrying an order is sacred too. Armies and goods are not. At sea, holiness protects a bearer from people, not a [[storms|storm]]: see [[runner-lost-at-sea]] and [[runner-rescued-at-sea]].

The first letter you send to a city also opens [[contact]] with it for good.

## Letters crossing water {#messengers-crossing-the-sea}

A messenger cannot swim any more than a Runner can. When a letter's road crosses open water it waits in your own port for a ship instead — see [[sea]] for how the crossing actually works.

If an order Runner is waiting for a ship, open its dispatch to see the sealed order's unit, destination and mission. Your Notifications history keeps those details readable after the Runner moves. Only your own order is shown; another Wanax's Runner does not reveal its envelope.

gossip:
**Gossip (🗣)** is what travellers, merchants and soldiers say about the world beyond your sight — who rules where, what happened, which cities exist.

Rumour is useful but blurred:

- it tells you **that** a city exists and **roughly** where, deliberately imprecise;
- it can be old news;
- it does **not** give you [[contact]] — you still have to reach a city before you can write to it or trade with it.

## Discovering and passing rumours {#gossip-details}

Rumours appear in the same list as your notifications, newest first, each marked **Rumour** with a 🗣 so you can tell what you have only heard from what happened to you. The rulers you know are listed under **Known** in Diplomacy.

Use gossip to decide where to send a [[messengers|messenger]] or a scout; use [[sight]] and [[contact]] to act on it.

## What becomes news, and how far it travels {#gossip-how-rumours-spread}

**Great events** — a city [[occupation|occupied]], annexed, sacked, burned, starved into surrender or collapsed — become rumours that the rulers nearby hear. **Small events** — a mine opened, a [[colonies|colony]] founded, goods flowing between two cities — are told only by those who saw them.

News moves with people. When your [[messengers|messenger]] reaches another ruler's city, you hear what that ruler has heard. Great news passes from ruler to ruler, at most **four** tellings from where it happened. Small news passes only from the eyewitness, once; whoever hears it second-hand does not pass it on. You hear each rumour at most once, however many ways it reaches you, and old news stops travelling. News received more than 72 real hours ago is no longer passed on. <!-- src: server/internal/gossip/gossip.go PropagateOnContact, maxMajorHops -->

## True divine omens {#gossip-divine-omens}

An omen is a true observation when it is given, rather than a promise about the future. It may point to a [[storms|storm]], metal-bearing ground, or the current balance between food production and the townsfolk's meals. When there is no true sign to convey, [[gods|the gods]] remain silent. Later movement or work can change what was observed.

Higher [[kharis]] brings signs more often, from farther away, and with more detail. Below 5 the minimum gap is 12 game days; at 5, 30, 60 and 90 it falls to 8, 5, 3 and 2 game days. These are minimum gaps, not promises that a sign will appear. A modest temple cannot reach the highest standing: a level 3 temple can support Kharis 100. Near Kharis 90 or above, a rare urgent coastal storm omen can name a concrete hex; it never gives an exact force, future arrival time or route. Low standing never reveals that precise hex. Names of unknown cities are not revealed.

Omens have their own star marker in Notifications. Your own goods movements are not told back to you as trade rumours.

telling-of-places:
No one draws maps. Instead you **tell of places**. A [[runners|letter]] to another Wanax can tell of one named place, such as a [[rivers|river]], a mountain massif, a [[semi-desert|desert]] or a [[cedar-forest|cedar forest]], or of a city your people have seen. When the Runner arrives, the place appears on the reader's map in grey, as [[sight|memory]].

## What a story carries {#telling-of-places-what}

A story is never false, and it carries **only what your own people have seen**. If they saw three hexes of a long river, the story tells of those three hexes and nothing more. It is fixed when the Runner leaves. Anything you see afterwards stays with you.

A city in the story is described **as you last saw it**: its name, its ruler, its walls and its size. If it has changed hands since then, the reader will not know until someone sees it again.

Only a **named** place can be told of, because a place gets its name from whoever reaches it first (see [[naming-the-land]]). You can also tell of any city your people have seen. You cannot pass on what you were only told yourself, because a story has to be first-hand.

## What the reader gets {#telling-of-places-reader}

The told hexes show as grey memory with the note **"Told by Wanax X"**. Told ground opens **no trade, gifts or founding**: a [[trade|trade offer]] needs your own people to have reached the city. You can still send a letter there. If your own people see the ground later, your own memory replaces the story.

A story costs nothing and is not counted. If you want payment for it, ask in the letter. A Runner lost at sea loses its story with it.

## Telling of places {#telling-of-places-how}

In the city panel, choose **Tell of a place** under the letter, then send. In [[keryx|Keryx]]: `keryx message --to Korinth --text "Hear of this" --tell-place "Dark Wood"`. `keryx places` shows which places you can tell of and who has told you of others. <!-- src: server/api/handlers/place_story.go buildStory; server/internal/messenger/story.go deliverStoryTx -->

stances:
A **stance** tells a unit how to behave where it stands. Set it in the order menu when you march, or later from **War → Forces** with the stance selector and **Set** — which, for a unit in the field, is an order carried by [[runners|Runner]].

## Choosing a stance {#stances-details}

- **fortify** — dig in and hold this ground. The unit cannot move until the stance is cleared. [[battle]] participation stays at 90%.
- **storm** — assault posture against an adjacent city. The wall stops only half as many hits, but the defenders’ hits on your men count one and a half times ([[battle]]).
- **sentry** — watch this ground. The unit spots marches passing **within 2 hexes** and **intercepts** enemy [[transfers|caravans]] there, checked every game day. [[messengers|Messengers]] are never stopped. You are alerted when it catches something.

## Changing stance by Runner {#stances-a-unit-already-on-the-march}

You can give a marching unit a stance too — from **War → Forces**, or by picking a stance when you right-click a new destination for it. The order goes by [[runners|Runner]], and the Runner has to **catch up** with the unit: the game tells you where and roughly when it will reach it. From then the unit carries the stance, and it takes hold **where the unit stops** — fortify digs in there, sentry watches that ground, storm goes into the assault it is marching on. A stance never turns a unit off its road.

If the unit is too far ahead for any Runner to overtake, the Runner follows it to its destination and the stance applies where it stopped.

## Taking a shared catchment hex {#stances-taking-a-shared-hex}

A unit in **fortify** or **sentry** on a hex that a neighbouring city holds lets you take it by placing a [[citizens|citizen]] there — see [[catchment]]. A unit that is only passing through does not.

## Sentry and sieges {#stances-sentry-is-a-siege}

Forces in sentry on the hexes an enemy city works stop those hexes feeding it. Hold that long enough and the city gives up without a battle — see [[sieges]].

## The cost of a watch {#stances-a-watch-is-not-free}

A unit standing in the field eats more than one in garrison, with nothing to forage ([[upkeep]]). Post watches where they buy you warning time — on the roads an enemy would take — not everywhere. See [[defence]].

From the command line there are also dedicated watch orders: a ship on **patrol** at a coastal hex, which comes home by itself after **24 game days** on station, and a land unit **posted** as a forward watch until you recall it ([[keryx]]).

## Battle retreat orders {#stances-when-to-retreat}

When your men break off a battle is set in two places:

- **For the whole realm** — **War → Forces → When to retreat**, or `keryx retreat-default` ([[keryx]]). Choose **Hold to the last man** or **Retreat when it goes badly** (the default). The latter withdraws when 20% of the side’s starting strength remains. Every unit carries this into a battle it enters. It applies at once, since no one has to carry it anywhere, but only to battles entered from then on — a battle already under way keeps what its forces brought.
- **For one unit, in the battle it is fighting now** — the **this battle** selector on its card in **War → Forces** (it only appears while the unit is fighting), or `keryx unit retreat-order`. Choose the same two modes. It overrides the realm setting for that battle only. On a field unit it is an order, and travels by [[runners|Runner]].

In Keryx, use `--hold-to-last-man` or `--when-losing` with either command. Fixed loss percentages are no longer a choice.

sieges:
A siege threatens a city by holding the ground that feeds it.

## Starving a city out {#sieges-starving-a-city-out}

You do not need to break a wall. Put forces in **sentry** ([[stances]]) on the hexes a city works, and those hexes stop feeding it. Hold it until the city's food need has gone uncovered for **30 game days in a row**, and **it capitulates on its own** — without a battle. The wall is never breached; the belly gives up.

This is the most powerful thing in the game that needs no [[bronze]], and the reason a city that cannot feed itself from its own [[catchment]] is fragile however thick its walls are.

## Blockaded catchment hexes {#sieges-blockaded-hexes}

A hex held by a hostile watch is **blockaded**: your people standing there produce nothing for you while it lasts. You are told when a hex is blockaded and when it is freed.

## Taking a catchment hex {#sieges-taking-a-hex}

Where two cities' [[catchment|catchments]] overlap, the hex belongs to the city with [[citizens|citizens]] on it. A rival with a unit of their own in [[stances|fortify or sentry]] there can take it by placing a citizen: your citizens on it go home to your idle pool, your building on it becomes theirs at the same level, and a build you had queued there is cancelled and refunded. You get a **HexTaken** notification naming who took it and how many of your citizens are idle. Take it back the same way. If your own unit is standing guard on the hex, it must be defeated first.

## Responding to a siege {#sieges-when-it-happens-to-you}

You get a notification when a siege begins and when it is lifted. The City drawer carries a red banner naming who holds the approaches, and the map draws a dark cloud over the city.

What helps:

- full [[food|city food stores]] — the city eats its [[grain|grain]], [[fish|fish]] and [[oil|oil]] while the fields are cut off;
- food brought in from outside by [[transfers|transfer]] — if it can get through;
- a relief force that drives the besiegers off the hexes ([[battle]]).

If the city falls, see [[occupation]].

upkeep:
Soldiers eat **food: [[grain|grain]], [[fish|fish]] and [[oil|oil]]**, shared in proportion to the stocks available. Professionals also need **silver pay** every game day.

## Soldiers’ and crews’ pay {#military-pay}

**Field pay starts at 1 silver per 100 people per game day**, the wage of a worker. [[spearmen]] draw **0 silver**. A full hundred-man [[elite-infantry]] cohort draws **1.5 silver in the field and 0.75 at home**; a full [[war-chariot|chariot]] cohort draws **4 in the field and 2 at home**. Remaining men determine the bill, so a half-strength cohort draws half the pay.

**Home pay is always half field pay.** Garrison, repairs and fitting out count as home. Marching, posted troops, freight missions and soldiers embarked aboard a ship count as field service. Soldiers’ pay does not flow back into the city: the stated silver bill is what the city loses.

**Only [[war-galley]] rowers draw silver pay**, per surviving crew member at 1 silver per hundred in the field and half at home. A full War Galley (50 rowers) draws **0.5 silver in the field / 0.25 at home**. [[galley|Galleys]] and [[emporos|Emporoi]] draw **0 silver**, in port and at sea, and cannot [[semi-desert|desert]] for lack of silver. Carried soldiers draw their own field pay in addition to their ship’s crew.

### Unpaid forces and laid-up crews {#military-pay-2}

If you cannot pay, you are warned first; if it goes on, **men desert**, and a unit that loses everyone is gone. Recruit professionals only when you can keep paying them. A spearman cohort costs 12 grain and 100 people to recruit, with no silver.

An empty ship **Laid up** in its own [[harbour|Harbour]] costs **0 silver and 0 ship food**. Its crew has returned to the city’s population and eats as civilians. **Fit out** drafts that crew back immediately; home-rate ship food and, for a War Galley, half field pay resume while the ship spends three game days preparing. See [[sea]].

## Food for soldiers and rowers {#military-food}

Soldiers use the same three [[food|food goods]] as the people. [[livestock]] remains emergency food for civilians, not an army ration. A full spearman cohort uses **0.5 food per game day in garrison**, or **1 food in the field**. A unit in garrison eats from its city. **A unit in the field eats double**, and there is nothing to forage — so a long march, a [[sieges|siege]] line or a posted watch is a real cost ([[stances]]). <!-- src: server/internal/combat/upkeep.go upkeepFieldGrainFactor -->

**Rowers eat like soldiers.** A ship feeds its actual surviving crew: each rower uses the same food as a spearman. A full Galley (20 rowers) uses **0.10 food per game day in port** or **0.20 on a mission**; a War Galley (50) uses **0.25 / 0.50**, and an Emporos (10) **0.05 / 0.10**. Fewer surviving rowers eat less. Garrisoned ships, repairs and fitting out use the port ration. Marching, posted and freighting ships use the field ration, including a freight route's wait between legs. A laid-up hull has no ship ration because its crew has returned to the city. Ship pay follows the home and field rates above.

### Military hunger and early ship returns {#military-food-2}

A unit that runs out of food **starves**: it loses men each game day, and can starve to nothing. A ship turns home when its remaining food is only enough for the way back, unless it can reach a city for provisions. Its owner hears the reason when it reaches home, not from far away; see [[ship-food-return]].

The Notifications archive marks when a city’s garrison starts losing men to hunger and when it stops. A recovery means that loss has stopped; it does not promise food for every soldier who may arrive later. Produce more food or send food by [[transfers|transfer]].

## Escort upkeep before founding {#upkeep-before-you-settle}

Your [[horde|Nomadic Host]]'s spearman escort draws no silver. The silver reserve you carry goes into the city when you settle; it does not limit how long the escort can travel.

## Keeping forces affordable {#upkeep-the-rule-of-thumb}

Your army is only as large as your economy can feed and pay. Build [[food]] surplus before you build cohorts, and [[silver]] income before expanding your professional forces ([[units|Forces]]).
